563
00:22:44.080 --> 00:22:47.580
How much of the parkour scene was scripted and choreographed?

564
00:22:48.120 --> 00:22:51.920
Well, I reached out to Paul Lieberstein, who wrote and directed this episode.

565
00:22:52.280 --> 00:22:56.920
Because in the script, it really just says they parkour through the office.

566
00:22:57.400 --> 00:23:06.020
And he said that the direction he gave Steve, Rain, and Ed was just a basic route to move through so that the camera could capture everything.

567
00:23:06.820 --> 00:23:11.280
He said, obviously, none of them had any parkour skills.

568
00:23:11.280 --> 00:23:13.720
They did no parkour training whatsoever.

569
00:23:14.160 --> 00:23:14.260
What?

570
00:23:14.400 --> 00:23:15.020
I'm shocked.

571
00:23:15.020 --> 00:23:17.140
It was not choreographed.

572
00:23:17.860 --> 00:23:23.480
He just told them, you can't do anything wrong, and your goal is to just mess up the room as much as possible.

573
00:23:24.380 --> 00:23:24.860
Achieved.

574
00:23:25.460 --> 00:23:29.140
Yet, he said the first take was really kind of underwhelming.

575
00:23:29.800 --> 00:23:37.260
So, they reset the office, and he said on the next take, he told them all to shout parkour as they went.

576
00:23:37.420 --> 00:23:41.020
So, whenever they attempted a move, they were supposed to shout parkour.

577
00:23:41.140 --> 00:23:45.320
So, they would do like a sloppy somersault off the back of a desk and go, parkour!

578
00:23:45.320 --> 00:23:52.620
Yes, and he said that ended up being the thing that gave it this really kind of weird energy that was very funny.

579
00:23:52.980 --> 00:24:04.820
So, yeah, I thought that was great, actually, because I thought a lot of the comedy came from the dichotomy between their very confident parkour and their horrible skills.

580
00:24:05.520 --> 00:24:11.620
Well, Jim has a great talking head that sort of explains what's happening, and he's like, you know, this was this internet sensation of 2004.

581
00:24:11.620 --> 00:24:13.260
It was in one of the Bond filMs.

582
00:24:13.780 --> 00:24:16.160
Lady, I think it was in Casino Royale.

583
00:24:16.880 --> 00:24:17.760
Hear me out.

584
00:24:17.820 --> 00:24:18.880
I love Daniel Craig.

585
00:24:19.060 --> 00:24:19.600
Yeah, same.

586
00:24:19.860 --> 00:24:24.540
And he had this great scene with actor named Sebastian Faucon.

587
00:24:24.880 --> 00:24:25.280
Okay.

588
00:24:25.760 --> 00:24:28.920
And, I mean, it was so cool.

589
00:24:29.180 --> 00:24:31.480
Talk about a choreographed fight scene.

590
00:24:31.480 --> 00:24:36.760
Well, isn't so much of the Matrix kind of rooted in parkour?

591
00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:37.660
Am I crazy?

592
00:24:38.020 --> 00:24:39.140
What part?

593
00:24:39.460 --> 00:24:40.480
What part?

594
00:24:40.780 --> 00:24:42.760
They're leaping all over stuff.

595
00:24:44.450 --> 00:24:45.050
No?

596
00:24:45.630 --> 00:24:49.770
Is it because in true parkour, you're really doing it?

597
00:24:49.930 --> 00:24:51.630
You don't have cables and stuff.

598
00:24:51.810 --> 00:24:55.330
Yeah, and you can't suspend time as a bullet goes past you.

599
00:24:55.690 --> 00:24:56.090
Right.

600
00:24:56.390 --> 00:24:57.650
That's not parkour.

601
00:24:58.750 --> 00:25:06.510
Well, I talked to Randy Cordray about this, and he said that that video that Jim is playing,

602
00:25:06.810 --> 00:25:14.930
that was a clip of an actual amateur filmmaker student from UCLA doing parkour.

603
00:25:15.510 --> 00:25:15.850
Oh.

604
00:25:16.030 --> 00:25:17.530
They licensed it for $300.

605
00:25:18.210 --> 00:25:25.750
And then we had a new graphic artist for Season 6 named Ryan Cosgrove, and he built a fakie

606
00:25:25.750 --> 00:25:27.810
YouTube-esque computer background.

607
00:25:28.010 --> 00:25:29.990
And so that's what you're seeing.

608
00:25:29.990 --> 00:25:35.230
He built that video into, like, a fakie video player.

609
00:25:35.450 --> 00:25:35.990
Nice.

610
00:25:35.990 --> 00:25:38.230
And that's what plays on Jim's computer.

611
00:25:38.410 --> 00:25:40.010
I think that kind of stuff is so cool.

612
00:25:40.370 --> 00:25:43.170
I clocked a bunch of props in this episode.

613
00:25:43.170 --> 00:25:46.410
I feel like Phil Shea was busy in the cold open alone.

